# Review: Java framework changes of the 0.17.0 DONTWAIT campaign — contract adaptation vs workaround vs spec gap

You are a codex sub-agent under a Claude supervisor doing a READ-ONLY conformance review. Do not edit any file except your deliverable; do not run gates that modify trees; never touch `core/**`, `bindings/**`, any `doc/spec/**`, `framework/doc/framework/**`, `doc/site/**`. Context: the framework worked end to end on Core 0.15/0.16 (baseline `seven-samples-green-v1` = `4bad5ac979`, 2026-08-29, all 7 samples green in 4 languages). Core 0.17.0 changed the contracts: SEND/REQUEST DONTWAIT return a wait token on BACKPRESSURED and the binding async terminal resubmits on WRITABLE (`50d77800f2`, D-B85 `7d8205a028`); `ZLINK_POLLCOMPLETION` is level-triggered and the public poller is the sole completion-drain owner; ROUTER submit to an unknown RID = `ZLINK_SUBMIT_NOT_FOUND`+`ENOENT`; ROUTER RID duplicate policy HANDOVER with reciprocal collapse by RID comparison (core socket README §4). The user's expectation: the ONLY framework changes needed are adaptations to those contract changes; every other change is suspect — either a legitimate pre-existing framework bug that the new Core exposed, or a workaround that hides a root cause (in Core, a binding, or the framework) and risks a spec gap. Your job: for EVERY commit listed below, read the diff (`git show <hash>`) and its worklog summary in `doc/plan/c016-worklog/*.md` (grep the hash), then classify it as exactly one of: (A) required 0.17.0 contract adaptation — cite the Core/binding spec clause (`core/doc/spec/**`, `bindings/doc/spec/**`) that requires it; (B) legitimate framework defect fix — cite the framework spec clause (`framework/doc/framework/common/spec/server/**`) the OLD code violated and explain why it was latent before 0.17.0; (C) workaround/mitigation — the change makes a symptom disappear without the cause being established (budget/timeout increases, retries, reordering "to be safe", fixture-only changes that hide runtime behaviour); name the most likely real cause and the evidence needed to confirm it; (D) spec gap — the change makes behaviour diverge from a spec clause (cite it) or invents a rule no spec has; (E) harness/build/sample-only, no runtime effect. PARITY CHECK is mandatory for every (B)/(C)/(D): find how the cpp implementation (and node) handles the same situation — cpp and node pass all gates — and state whether cpp/node needed an equivalent change (if cpp did NOT need it, explain precisely why dotnet/java did: a genuine structural difference, or a symptom of a different root cause). Verdict per commit: KEEP / RE-FIX AT ROOT (where) / REVERT. Be concrete: file:line, spec clause ids, and what the cpp code does. Deliverable: a Korean markdown report with one table row per commit (hash, one-line, class, spec clause, cpp/node parity finding, verdict) followed by a "재수정 권고" section ordered by risk, and a "스펙 gap 후보" section. 2 h cap.

## Commits to review (java + kotlin), oldest first
- `e65abaf7ac` framework/java: transition runtime to the 0.16.0 pull-completion model (context)
- `4d263e66b9` framework: route DONTWAIT sends through the binding async path and drain WRITABLE on POLLOUT (java part)
- `4af4f66f31` framework/java: classify ClientServer control by isRequest(), not numeric requestSeq
- `af7afd28e7` (java part) ZoneWorld crash-boundary port
- `2b1d0c794c` framework/java/tests: wait for the create turn's drain barrier before sealing the relocation in the post-cut tests (test-only: does it hide a runtime ordering defect?)
- `ed156f5983` framework/java/samples: DeliveryDispatch courier offer deadline after the status publish (+ `ec2ee1ea50` parity port to cpp/dotnet/node — a sample semantics change: was the OLD behaviour a sample bug or was it exposing a framework latency regression (status publish taking longer on 0.17)? check timings in the summaries)
- `6682ae0db1` framework/java: treat a synchronous NOT_CONNECTED on shutdown-time session unbind as an idempotent unbind — is NOT_CONNECTED at unbind a spec-defined idempotent case (cite `bindings/doc/spec`/core socket spec) or a swallowed error?
- `dc9fe76100` framework/java: keep the STREAM liveness cycle alive when a heartbeat ping hits a closing transport (catches ZlinkSubmitException) — swallowed error or spec-conformant?
- `36c310ffef` framework/java: poll SUB sockets with POLLIN only instead of claiming a completion queue (compare with dotnet `4644af9d03` and cpp)
- `424b15684c` framework/java: admit bound-session sends (command 36) asynchronously so the mesh poller owner keeps draining completions
- `7b0590183d` framework/java: send STREAM control frames asynchronously so the receive owner never waits on the state lane
- `efad74ef94` framework/java: bound-session push no longer waits on the STREAM socket state lane from the caller thread
  For the last three: these are "state lane join on the caller thread" fixes. Parity: cpp has no such lane joins (single-threaded owner model?) — explain whether the Java lane design itself violates the execution contract (`01-execution/02-handler-turn-and-execution-gate.ko.md:15-29,466-482`) and whether the three fixes are complete (list remaining synchronous `inStateLane(...).join()` / `admissionTimeout()` sites reachable from infrastructure owners — grep `ZLinkJavaStreamSocket.java`, `ZLinkJavaRawMeshNode.java`, `ZLinkJavaSocketBacked.java`).
- `9e76d053e5` samples/kotlin GameQuest runner; `83dfe33fb5` ZoneWorld runner; `cb22cf6ae5` sample sleeps removal (E unless they change scenario semantics — check the TicTacToe WatchService wait and the DeliveryDispatch Clock injection keep the same assertions)

Also: the two known-failing `ZLinkJavaRawMeshNodeM6ATest` cases (`:1400`, `:1439`, "descriptor fence / inproc close") were left as pre-existing (C). Read them and say whether they encode a spec rule the campaign's Java changes now contradict.

